  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nMid-level ridging is expected across the Central Plains through \nmuch of the forecast period. Current 500-hPa analysis shows a \nridge axis centered over the area, with a weak shortwave moving \nthrough the flow to the west. Energy from this shortwave has led\nto light showers developing across eastern KS and western MO, \nhowever, with a lot of dry air present in the lowest 10k ft, \nonly a few sprinkles have been able to make it to the ground. \nCloud cover, tied to the shortwave, is present over the \nsouthwestern portion of the CWA and is expected to spread across\nthe remainder of the area through the afternoon and evening, \nallowing temperatures to peak around 70F today. The ridge axis \nshifts west this evening and another shortwave is progged to \nmove over the peak of the ridge and into the Central Plains. \nThis will once again allow showers to form across central and \neastern KS. The shortwave is expected to be weakening as it \napproaches western MO, and with dry air still in place in the \nlowest 10k ft, a few sprinkles remain possible, but it is \nunlikely that any meaningful precipitation will be seen across \nthe CWA.\n\nRidging will remain centered just to the west of the local area \non Wednesday, as the shortwave moves to the east. At the \nsurface, high pressure over the Great Lakes will result in light\nsoutherly winds. Weak WAA and mid-level height rises will yield\nhigh temperatures in the mid-70s. By Thursday, the ridge axis \nwill once again be centered overhead, with the height rises and \nWAA bringing temperatures into the upper 70s through Saturday. \nOn Saturday, a trough will eject across the northern Rockies \nresulting in lee cyclogenesis in MT. This system will lift \nnortheast into Canada and its cold front will advance towards \nthe Central Plains, but is not expected to make it this far \nsouth before stalling out. Although it will remain stalled north\nof the area, forcing along the front may be enough to see a few\nshowers on Sunday across far northwestern MO, although the \nchances remain low (15-30%) given the presence of dry low- level\nair. Another trough ejection on Sunday should be enough force \nthe cold front across the area on Monday, bringing high \ntemperatures back towards seasonal normals in the low-to-mid \n60s.",
